    <string name="about_title">About TreeCalc</string>
    <string name="about_functionality">TreeCalc is a calculator that turns a typed infix expression into postfix (Reverse Polish) notation and an expression tree, then evaluates that tree.</string>
    <string name="about_goal">The goal is to display the expression tree for demonstration purposes — so you can see how operators nest, not only the final number.</string>
    <string name="about_source">The original desktop implementation, which I created for my sons in 2005, can be found in the repository below.</string>
    <string name="about_source_url">https://github.com/lkovari/Calculator</string>
    <string name="about_author">László Kővári © 2005 – 2026</string>
    <string name="about_email">laszlo.kovary@gmail.com</string>
    <string name="help_title">Help</string>
    <string name="help_swipe_title">Switching screens</string>
    <string name="help_swipe_body">The calculator and the expression tree live side by side. From either screen, swipe left or swipe right to show the other. It does not matter which screen you are on — a swipe in either direction always opens the other view.</string>
    <string name="help_bases_title">Numerical systems</string>
    <string name="help_bases_body">Choose base 2, 8, 10, or 16. Decimal supports fractions and scientific functions. Binary, octal, and hexadecimal use integers. Letters A–F are available in hexadecimal only.</string>
    <string name="help_convert_title">Converting numerical systems</string>
    <string name="help_convert_body">Type a number, press =, then choose the numerical system you want (2, 8, 10, or 16). The value is shown in the system you selected. For example, decimal 10 becomes A in hexadecimal and 1010 in binary.</string>
    <string name="help_equals_title">Equals and the tree</string>
    <string name="help_equals_body">Press = to evaluate. The result appears on the calculator. Swipe to the expression tree screen to inspect the postfix form. Tap a postfix token to jump to that evaluation step on the tree, and expand or collapse tree nodes. Press Tst to fill in a nested sample expression as if you had typed it, then press =.</string>
    <string name="help_angle_title">Angle units</string>
    <string name="help_angle_body">Sine, cosine, and tangent use degrees by default, so sin(90) is 1. Press Deg/Rad to switch to radians when you need sin(π/2). The current unit is shown next to the numerical system.</string>
